2026-11-22 20:23:43 - Agent_20261122202113 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed the 2 CodeQL results for py/s3transfer-tempfile-overview (CWE-377/CWE-732) in the s3transfer package.

1. s3transfer/compat.py:22-34 (rename_file shim, incl. sink at line 32) — FALSE POSITIVE. The flagged "sink Attribute" is just the module-level function alias `rename_file = os.rename` on POSIX plus a Windows shim that removes the destination file before renaming. There is no temp-file creation, no permission manipulation and no attacker-controlled path derivation here; it is a purely defensive/portability wrapper used for the final atomic rename.

2. s3transfer/upload.py:675-677 (CreateMultipartUploadTask submission inside _submit_multipart_request) — FALSE POSITIVE. The flagged region is only creation of an upload task with bucket/key/extra_args from `transfer_future.meta.call_args`; uploads never create local temp files (bodies come from the user-supplied file object), so neither CWE-377 nor CWE-732 applies.

3. NEW finding (true positive, reported independently): s3transfer/utils.py get_temp_filename()/open() (lines 330-338, used by s3transfer/download.py:169 and 188 for DownloadFilenameOutputManager) — the temporary download file name is derived from the destination path plus an 8-hex-digit `random.choice` suffix generated with the global non-cryptographic `random` module (not mkstemp/tempfile), and the file is then created via plain `self.open(filename, 'wb')` which follows symlinks, creates with default 0644 umask permissions and does not use O_EXCL. An attacker with write access to the download directory (shared/world-writable dirs, or a predictable-path target) can pre-create or symlink a matching temp name to redirect/symlink-overwrite writes and read the partially downloaded content, i.e., insecure temp file handling plus world-readable perms. Suggested fix: use tempfile.mkstemp in the target directory with 0600 permissions.

Total submitted: 3 findings (1 tp, 2 fp).
